 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "THE TYNE BRIDGE. Opened in 1928 and the symbol of the city. Its builders, Dorman Long of Middlesbrough, went on to make the Sydney Harbour Bridge, which looks rather like it. Seven bridges cross the Tyne between Newcastle and Gateshead within about a mile, and every Geordie has a favourite."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 44,
   "text": "GATESHEAD MILLENNIUM BRIDGE. Opened in 2001, the world's first tilting bridge. When a ship comes, the whole arch swings up like an eyelid, and it is nicknamed 'The Blinking Eye'. It really does blink."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 9,
   "text": "THE NEW CASTLE. Robert Curthose, son of William the Conqueror, put up a wooden fort here in 1080 on the site of a Roman one, and the town is named for that 'new castle'. The stone keep dates from 1172 and is one of the best-preserved Norman keeps in the country. A railway line runs right past it."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 15,
   "text": "GREY STREET. Often voted one of the finest streets in England. It curves down the hill in honey stone to Grey's Monument, a column for the Prime Minister who passed the Great Reform Act in 1832. Locals use it as a meeting point: 'meet you at the Monument' is the city's whole diary."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 46,
   "text": "THE TOWN MOOR. 1,000 acres of open grass in the middle of a city, bigger than Hyde Park and Hampstead Heath put together. The Freemen of Newcastle have the right to graze their cattle here, and some still do, which is why the cows have a better address than you."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 4,
   "text": "THE QUAYSIDE. In the Middle Ages this was the busiest bit of the city: coal, wool and salt left from here to everywhere. On Sunday mornings there's a market with cheese, crafts, and a sausage roll that could convert the Pope."
  },
  {
   "x": 57,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "THE SAGE. A great curving ripple of glass and steel, opened in 2004, that looks like a silver armadillo that learned music. It is home to the Royal Northern Sinfonia. The concert hall is called Hall One. The architect gave it no other name. It is exactly what it says."
  },
  {
   "x": 56,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "THE BALTIC. Not a sea, a flour mill. It was Rank Hovis's mill, built 1950, and became a very large gallery for contemporary art in 2002. Locals call it the Baltic, as the fashion was to name a mill after a sea, and the sea after a mill. There is a viewing box on the roof."
  },
  {
   "x": 65,
   "y": 57,
   "text": "THE ANGEL OF THE NORTH. Antony Gormley's steel angel stands on a hill beside the A1, 20 metres tall with a wingspan of 54 metres, wider than a jumbo jet. Made of weathering steel that goes a deep rust red, and visited by about 150,000 people each year. She was put up in 1998 and some people hated her. Nobody does now."
  }
 ],
